1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:03,760
Module 1, Video 1.5. Who writes? Who checks?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:04,700
Who accepts?

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:13,520
Well, the core premise here is that the AI assistant may draft, but a named person accepts, amends, or sets aside each proposal,

4
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:17,040
and, you know, only what was accepted actually stands.

5
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:23,600
Right. So an AI assistant can, um, draft a register of requirements in an afternoon, which is,

6
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:29,180
I mean, real help for your program. But if a machine wrote it, who is answerable for it?

7
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:32,200
Well, your minister will ask that exact question.

8
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:33,100
I'm sure they will.

9
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:40,680
Yeah. The assistant provides rapid drafting, which is great, but it does not remove the requirement for a human official to remain accountable.

10
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:45,560
So how does the, uh, SDD method answer this requirement for accountability?

11
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,640
The AI assistant proposes, but it never decides.

12
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:55,400
A named person rules line by line. They must, um, categorize each proposal.

13
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,880
So one, accepted. Okay. Accepted. Two, amended.

14
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,160
And three, set aside.

15
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:07,180
The document holds only what was accepted by someone who can, you know, be asked about it later.

16
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,820
Right, because inventing plausible answers is what an assistant does best.

17
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,920
So what happens when an official's text is silent on a necessary detail?

18
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:21,840
Well, the SDD method dictates a gap is recorded as a question with an owner.

19
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:25,800
It is never filled with an invented answer or...

20
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:27,340
Or a guessed figure.

21
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:33,360
Exactly. Never a guessed figure. This ensures there's one less fault hidden in the service.

22
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:34,440
I see.

23
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:40,140
To structure this oversight within your ministry, what are the three roles for every document?

24
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:41,140
Um,

25
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,840
someone writes, someone checks,

26
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,080
and a named person accepts.

27
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:48,700
We can observe this in Progressa.

28
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:52,040
Right. The fictional country of these examples, Progressa.

29
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:53,340
Yes, exactly.

30
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:58,980
In Progressa's PHEQA project, the writer and the assistant never accept alone.

31
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:04,100
The head of the registration desk sits at every review of something an officer will use.

32
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:11,360
So within PHEQA's project, in which specific areas does the AI assistant do the writing?

33
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,000
Uh, it handles four areas.

34
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:16,660
First, it extracts the register.

35
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,140
Second, it helps write shared settings and lists.

36
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:20,460
Okay.

37
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:23,620
Third, it drafts each goal as a story.

38
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,760
And fourth, it writes the application model, where it must completely refuse to guess.

39
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,680
So in each of those instances?

40
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:39,720
In each, yes. A named person, like the registrar, setting owner, or head of the ICT unit, accepts it.

41
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:42,780
To ensure those decisions remain within your organization,

42
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,420
what three things must we check on the supplier's table before the very first document is written?

43
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:50,260
You must check three things.

44
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:51,080
One,

45
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:53,460
no row ends with the assistant.

46
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:55,380
Makes sense. Two,

47
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,640
the writer never accepts alone, meaning two other people must agree.

48
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:03,460
And three, the person who will live with it is in the room.

49
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,160
Wait, what if the supplier is named under accepts?

50
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:11,120
Well, if the supplier or nobody is named under accepts,

51
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:14,340
that decision has not been given to anyone in your organization.

52
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:20,040
Right, which means things could easily end up siloed or, you know, costly to change.

53
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:22,700
An assistant may draft any document.

54
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:25,360
A named person rules on each line,

55
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,200
and only what that person accepted stands.
